<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for the OpenConvert host process under the hardened runtime.

  THE HOST IS NOT SANDBOXED, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. `03 §9` puts the trust
  boundary between the host and the engines, not around the application: the
  host reads the file the user pointed at, writes the output where the user
  asked, and spawns one confined worker per engine. Putting the host inside the
  App Sandbox would mean every path arriving through the CLI, a batch directory
  or a recipe had to come back through Powerbox, which is a different product.

  So the confinement that matters is applied to the CHILD, by
  `openconvert-os::macos`, and this file exists to keep the host's own privileges
  as small as a non-sandboxed process can be.

  ⚠️ UNVERIFIED. Every macOS claim in this project is reasoning rather than
  measurement (`docs/spec/TARGETS.md`), and that includes this file. It has
  never been through `codesign`, `spctl` or a notarization submission. The
  runbook that would settle it is the private macOS validation runbook.

  What is deliberately ABSENT matters more than what is present:

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
        Would let any unsigned dylib load into the host. Our engines are signed
        with the same Team ID, so validation should pass without it. If a build
        appears to need this, the correct fix is to sign the engine.

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
    com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
        Each removes a hardened-runtime protection outright. Neither has a
        reason here.

    com.apple.security.automation.apple-events
        The app scripts nothing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    WKWebView's JavaScript engine JITs. The web content runs in its own process
    (com.apple.WebKit.WebContent) with its own entitlements, but the host is
    what launches it, and a hardened-runtime host that cannot allow JIT is the
    usual cause of a blank window on first launch.

    This is the one protection knowingly relaxed, and it is relaxed for a
    process that -- per the CSP in tauri.conf.json -- loads no remote script,
    connects to no remote origin, and renders no remote frame.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
